  "id": "COMPOUND:433 - PROTEIN:6323",
  "verbalization": "Epirubicin is implicated in the regulation of DNA polymerase iota through a series of molecular interactions involving the NCI-H1650 cell line. The evidence suggests that epirubicin acts as a target for multiple compounds, including Isosinomenine, Arnebifuranone, and 16alpha-Acetoxycalotropin, all of which are known to target DNA polymerase iota. These findings indicate that epirubicin may exert its biological effects by modulating the activity of these compounds, which in turn influence DNA polymerase iota. The consistent presence of DNA polymerase iota as a downstream target across different compounds implies a potential mechanism of action involving the regulation of DNA polymerase iota through the interaction with these intermediates. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of epirubicin in orchestrating a network of molecular interactions that ultimately affect DNA polymerase iota, suggesting a broader pharmacological impact on DNA replication and repair processes.",
